h2. Summary

This RFC proposes changing the PPDB Solar System table baseline from the current model, where {{SSSource}} is tightly linked 1:1 to {{DiaSource}}, to a decoupled model in which Solar System tables are constructed from a persistent record of the measurements submitted to the MPC. The new baseline makes {{SSSource}} and {{SSObject}} independent of the current contents and identifier namespace of the PPDB {{DiaSource}} table, while preserving a lightweight association table for users who need to relate PPDB {{DiaSource}} detections to nearby predicted Solar System objects.

h2. Motivation

The current baseline assumes that Solar System detections accepted by the MPC can be mapped back to PPDB {{DiaSource}} rows and used to regenerate {{SSSource}} and {{SSObject}} daily. This assumption fails once Rubin data releases begin replacing previously submitted prompt-processing measurements with DRP-derived measurements. At that point, the MPC’s list of accepted source identifiers may contain a mix of PP-generated and DRP-generated IDs, while the PPDB {{DiaSource}} table contains only PP-generated rows.

This also creates problems for commissioning, early survey validation, source-based moving-object measurements before templates are available, and special processing campaigns. These measurements may be legitimate Rubin-submitted Solar System observations, but may not exist as PPDB {{DiaSource}} rows. As a result, the current baseline risks making the PPDB Solar System products incomplete, hard to regenerate, and difficult to explain to users.

h2. Proposed Change

Adopt a new PPDB Solar System table baseline with the following design:

# For every submission to the MPC, persist the full measurement record that was submitted, including the Source or DiaSource-like astrometric and photometric fields needed to reconstruct Solar System data products.
# When Rubin receives the MPC’s accepted association information, construct {{SSSource}} and {{SSObject}} from this persistent submitted-measurement store rather than from PPDB {{DiaSource}}.
# Extend {{SSSource}} to include the DiaSource-like astrometric and photometric columns that users currently obtain by joining {{SSSource}} to {{DiaSource}}.
# Break the hard schema dependency between {{DiaSource}} and {{SSSource}}.
# Remove {{ssObjectId}} from {{DiaSource}}.
# Treat all {{DiaSource}} rows as normal {{DiaObject}}-associated difference-image detections, rather than mutating them daily based on Solar System associations.
# Add a small daily-regenerated association table, tentatively named {{NearbyAsteroids}}, containing tuples such as:
{{diaSourceId, ssSourceId, distance}}
This table provides a user-facing way to discover which PPDB {{DiaSource}} rows were near predicted Solar System object positions without requiring {{DiaSource}} itself to carry Solar System state.

h2. Consequences

This change makes the PPDB Solar System products independent of whether a measurement originated in prompt processing, DRP, commissioning processing, source-based early processing, or a special run. It also removes the need for daily bulk updates to {{DiaSource}}, avoids rewriting or invalidating previously published alert identifiers, and allows the Solar System side of PPDB to be managed as a smaller, more self-contained data product.

The primary user-facing model becomes:

For the most up-to-date, best-quality, evergreen Rubin understanding of the Solar System, use the PPDB Solar System tables.

For a homogeneous, versioned, pipeline-consistent catalog with well-characterized systematics, use the most recent DRP Solar System data products.

This change may also allow the DRP side to retire the proposed “gold reprocessing” Solar System product and instead retain only the LSST-only Solar System catalog, reducing DRP complexity by removing the MPC from that processing flow.

h2. Implementation Notes

The submitted-measurement store should be append-oriented and immutable, keyed by the identifiers used in MPC submission and acceptance workflows. It must retain sufficient astrometric, photometric, provenance, and processing-context metadata to reconstruct {{SSSource}} and {{SSObject}} without consulting {{DiaSource}}.

{{SSSource}} should become measurement-complete for Solar System use cases. Its schema should include the fields needed for Solar System science queries without requiring a join to {{DiaSource}}.

{{NearbyAsteroids}} should be regenerated and bulk-loaded daily. It should remain intentionally narrow and should not be treated as the authoritative Solar System association state; that role belongs to {{SSSource}}/{{SSObject}}.

